For R<sub>1</sub>; 2x + y = 10 and x, y $$ \in $$ N possible values for x and y are : 


x = 1, y = 8    i.e.   (1, 8);


x = 2, y = 6    i.e    (2, 6);


x = 3, y = 4    i.e    (3, 4);


x = 4, y = 2    i.e    (4, 2) 


$$\therefore\,\,\,$$ R<sub>1</sub> = { (1, 8), (2, 6), (3, 4), (4, 2) }


$$\therefore\,\,\,$$ Range of R<sub>1</sub> is {2, 4, 6, 8}


R<sub>1</sub> is not symmetric.


R<sub>1</sub> is not transitive also as 


(3, 4), (4, 2) $$ \in $$ R , but (3, 2) $$ \notin $$ R<sub>1</sub>


For R<sub>2</sub> : x + 2y = 10 and x, y $$ \in $$ N


Possible values of x, and y are : 


x = 8, y= 1    i.e    (8, 1)


x = 6, y = 2    i.e    (6, 2)


x = 4, y = 3    i.e    (4, 3) and


x = 2, y = 4    i.e    (2, 4)


$$\therefore\,\,\,$$ R<sub>2</sub> = {(8, 1) (6, 2) (4, 3) (2, 4)}


$$\therefore\,\,\,$$ Range of R<sub>2</sub> = $$\left\{ {1,2,3,4} \right\}$$


R<sub>2</sub> is not symmetric and transitive

